#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221125 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing GTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/id #uuid "fb9baafb-4eca-593f-ad3e-34744fdfd3f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21684" :question/problem "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $\\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = 4(\\Delta x)^3 + (\\Delta x)^4.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\quad$ With step size $\\Delta x = 0.4,$ the global truncation error is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/assets/ee7115883733062534307c8ff2b1795c77d47d1e8dffeb357dcbe94e07e1f400.png)\n\n$\\quad$ With step size $\\Delta x = 0.2,$ the global truncation error is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/assets/3883232317dc9ab3e8888fab646fb593ca37585ef3367c5b3000be226962e512.png)\n\n$\\quad$ Decreasing from $\\Delta x = 0.4$ to $\\Delta x = 0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/assets/ea99ff6ac18f5bb8506edcecfe913b338b64d04e99532f67cec6eb94efc2e9fa.png)\n\n$\\quad$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/assets/d8eaeb8b6abf175f486560cf0d407fd97cd5180907c2db8e6436ccc7e4b09f04.png)" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^p\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately $k^p.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.4,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.4) & =4(0.4)^{3}+(0.4)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.064+1\\cdot 0.025\\,6 \\\\  & =0.256+0.025\\,6 \\\\  & =0.281\\,6 \\\\  & ≈0.282.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =4(0.2)^{3}+(0.2)^{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.008+1\\cdot 0.001\\,6 \\\\  & =0.032+0.001\\,6 \\\\  & =0.033\\,6 \\\\  & ≈0.034.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.4}{0.2} = 2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\nk^p = 2^p.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,6$ to $0.033\\,6.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.281\\,6}{0.033\\,6} = 8.380\\,95\\ldots \\approx  \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{8.381}.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈8.380\\,952… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(8.380\\,95…) \\\\ p & ≈3.067….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{3}.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = 4(\\Delta x)^3 + (\\Delta x)^4\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^3\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221126 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/id #uuid "c94bf874-2055-5ff3-83e3-b5740a1e14e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-21685" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.24,$ the observed local truncation error is $\\tau(0.24) = 0.8.$\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $\\tau(0.08) = 0.01.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method, rounded to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.24$ to $0.08.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.24}{0.08} = 3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\nk^{p+1} = 3^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.8$ to $0.01.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.8}{0.01} = 80.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈80 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(80) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.987… \\\\ p & ≈2.987….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221127 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/id #uuid "b9b0118d-5238-5644-a3d6-695d8f393efe" :question/math-academy-id "e-21686" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($\\tau$) represent a second-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(\\Delta x) = 3(\\Delta x)^2 + 4(\\Delta x)^5$\n\n- $\\tau(\\Delta x) = 0.6(\\Delta x)^3 + 2(\\Delta x)^4$\n\n- $e(\\Delta x) = 7(\\Delta x)^4 + 5(\\Delta x)^6$" :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O((\\Delta x)^p)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O((\\Delta x)^{p+1})\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option:\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(\\Delta x) = 3(\\Delta x)^2 + 4(\\Delta x)^5,$ the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $3(\\Delta x)^2 = 3(\\Delta x)^2.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $\\tau(\\Delta x) = 0.6(\\Delta x)^3 + 2(\\Delta x)^4,$ the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $0.6(\\Delta x)^3 = 0.6(\\Delta x)^{2+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(\\Delta x) = 7(\\Delta x)^4 + 5(\\Delta x)^6,$ the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $7(\\Delta x)^4 = 7(\\Delta x)^4.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221128 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"}] :question/id #uuid "de3c4be6-9d3d-5dcb-b1cf-567195c4ee63" :question/math-academy-id "e-21687" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $1.$\nSuppose that using step size $\\Delta x = 0.4$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $\\tau(0.4) \\approx 0.24.$\nIf we instead use a step size $\\Delta x = 0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14048125/assets/31f454d50de05da419a55dfcdd26ac8d7883635e6eb0b95367f9f80fb30643a5.png)" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\frac{0.4}{0.1} = 4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $1,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\nk^{1+1} = 4^2 = 16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $\\Delta x = 0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\n\\tau(0.1) \\approx \\frac{0.24}{16} = 0.015.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a064cf27-b75b-5e43-96b0-efebe92e7479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220460} {:answer/id #uuid "d3079631-4123-5f02-9f03-b26206188e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220461} {:answer/id #uuid "134ee36b-8546-51cd-bbfc-fbfae2b3c95c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220462} {:answer/id #uuid "7b63cdd9-58f9-51f7-8bf8-5a3c43519907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220463} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6a0180-06ef-53b7-9e1d-4662ba5050a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c6a0180-06ef-53b7-9e1d-4662ba5050a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220464} :answer-field/id #uuid "87eafb71-b874-5755-aae9-466ade2be33e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3f43810-818f-5e05-945e-483e5e28efaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-333692" :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{2}$\n- $e(Δx)=0.3Δx+2(Δx)^{2}$\n- $e(Δx)=9(Δx)^{2}+4(Δx)^{5}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.3Δx+2(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3Δx.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=9(Δx)^{2}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is  $9(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f634b4d1-a4f4-5784-bd1c-4a64e5a541c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327624} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa15dc3-bd0b-552a-90c0-73de2f1b4686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327625} {:answer/id #uuid "92dd784f-e1a6-58e1-ab66-aede639d09c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327626} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff0e7ec-9d2f-50b1-b9d3-a971d2b9e54e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327627} {:answer/id #uuid "9ecaa1b6-f9ec-5f8d-81c0-92cca7c416e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92dd784f-e1a6-58e1-ab66-aede639d09c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327626} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bf4d460-f9a9-54f3-ba46-00f04374de48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "799c4ad6-53eb-5ac5-9b2e-37fa2f8cd5ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-333693" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}$\n- $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2}$\n- $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}$" :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{2}=2(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $-3(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $(Δx)^{4}=(Δx)^{3+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35c6ff5f-b960-523f-a39b-4f6104cd1021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327630} {:answer/id #uuid "a38b2ea7-24b2-5c0c-a0ba-40e6f76e09f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327631} {:answer/id #uuid "d3eab7d5-bd8b-5c86-b790-356242997f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327632} {:answer/id #uuid "53dea72e-ba3e-5886-ae86-aef8d3a3ae9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327633} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae0eadc-d07c-5bc2-af9b-242397448209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a38b2ea7-24b2-5c0c-a0ba-40e6f76e09f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327631} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc8b53eb-b07f-5a25-a094-5bfe3aa79338" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07099fdd-1348-5e3a-a68e-3d7b94b8ddb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-333702" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08)=0.13.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.02,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.02)=0.002.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.08$ to $0.02.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.08}{0.02}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={4}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.13$ to $0.002.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.13}{0.002}=65.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{p+1} & ≈65 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{4}⁡(65) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.011… \\\\ p & ≈2.011….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94fe2a9b-29c8-5211-bef2-f4b10fc1da50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17.671" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220480} {:answer/id #uuid "b32c12ee-0f13-5d36-b9bb-1416a25f12f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.864" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220481} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b600e4-2504-5509-bc3b-bfb63908a91b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.168" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220482} {:answer/id #uuid "d3cf39d0-7528-53b7-b00b-09ec275e2c23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.769" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220483} {:answer/id #uuid "da08066e-0f6a-5879-93b2-e2dceeccc37a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3cf39d0-7528-53b7-b00b-09ec275e2c23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.769" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220483} :answer-field/id #uuid "de0ec9d9-bacd-5192-b393-fcd6f4bfdfe7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220479} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6b729fd-b6c3-5458-acfe-6fafd74d0bde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220486} {:answer/id #uuid "d46e53f7-419d-5058-8df6-5e46239f6e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.550" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220487} {:answer/id #uuid "41e4e18f-c674-5400-a4a8-cba407ac23df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220488} {:answer/id #uuid "533da575-aab6-5770-9666-441e92fe1c3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220489} {:answer/id #uuid "5f321f79-0b6d-584d-a3f1-608915700d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "533da575-aab6-5770-9666-441e92fe1c3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220489} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7615e5a-baaf-5e9d-a712-ad2473b24d8e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220485} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be04b902-6ca8-587b-a9b2-ebacd38c76c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220492} {:answer/id #uuid "d839be3b-a11f-5494-a791-1c46178c865d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220493} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ad944c-b98b-5a22-b3f0-8441eaf6e803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220494} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f9b33a-6c67-5070-b52a-9e00ee4a970f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.550" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220495} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8f3cf9-7a8a-5b15-8081-101da9a1e176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4ad944c-b98b-5a22-b3f0-8441eaf6e803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220494} :answer-field/id #uuid "720a82e7-b8aa-5ec0-8834-c9fb20c0cc34" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220491} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "133b4a60-34e9-535a-a4c9-beafc36c962c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220498} {:answer/id #uuid "36fcc14b-cd3f-5051-8a15-f7c286603177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220499} {:answer/id #uuid "4f0e081b-723c-5092-aa81-fe6aa24d9324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220500} {:answer/id #uuid "75da881e-89de-5a2c-9c67-bb235b5d4846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220501} {:answer/id #uuid "7d4eab11-e4e5-5275-8c61-f996ee21b654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d4eab11-e4e5-5275-8c61-f996ee21b654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220502} :answer-field/id #uuid "acff0c74-b1c7-528a-a02e-384b29fcab06" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36473f35-3093-5439-832b-c821da150814" :question/math-academy-id "q-333706" :question/problem "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=7(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the local truncation error is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.5$ to $Δx=0.25,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.5) & =7(0.5)^{4}-2(0.5)^{5} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 0.062\\,5-2\\cdot 0.031\\,25 \\\\  & =0.375.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.25) & =7(0.25)^{4}-2(0.25)^{5} \\\\  & =7\\cdot (0.003\\,906…)-2\\cdot (0.000\\,976…) \\\\  & =0.025\\,39… \\\\  & ≈0.025.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.25.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.25}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.375$ to $0.025\\,39….$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.375}{(0.025\\,39…)}=14.769\\,23…≈14.769.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p+1} & ≈14.769\\,23… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(14.769\\,23…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.884… \\\\ p & ≈2.884….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$3.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=7(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})=O\\,((Δx)^{3+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing GTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e62caed9-6645-5d59-932c-50f28b9970be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327637} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c01cf5-0d6a-55d2-bdb8-1f0f018f867d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327638} {:answer/id #uuid "1918ea97-72ce-5484-8de7-abf8e7e4e6bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327639} {:answer/id #uuid "200dde8a-5f17-56a5-8461-180348dae7ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327640} {:answer/id #uuid "66cb5a24-943d-556c-a533-94fb9aef2ede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9c01cf5-0d6a-55d2-bdb8-1f0f018f867d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327638} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4075b71-d2c9-5f16-b014-1b9c3f35515f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "133c2975-c93c-564e-ad25-860da7e0d9e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334542" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.12,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.12)=0.310.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.06)=0.078.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.12$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.12}{0.06}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={2}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.310$ to $0.078.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.310}{0.078}=3.974….\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈3.974… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(3.974…) \\\\ p & ≈1.990….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the approximate order of the method is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327642 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing GTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dc36703-75eb-5afe-859b-2ce2866899f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327644} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b93d36-6bac-58a7-a664-69247c11a284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327645} {:answer/id #uuid "7f29c5fd-4a78-5841-8d8e-796392a8974c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327646} {:answer/id #uuid "4983bc87-8140-5aa7-bda9-5c0258088161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327647} {:answer/id #uuid "5916280f-0472-568a-8079-bf285cc81e4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6b93d36-6bac-58a7-a664-69247c11a284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327645} :answer-field/id #uuid "58b9225f-157c-553b-be20-43c7488467c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "500e48c1-7345-566d-9368-b7d69cfccd17" :question/math-academy-id "q-334544" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.18,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.18)=0.740.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.06)=0.090.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.18$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.18}{0.06}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={3}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.740$ to $0.090.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.740}{0.090}=8.222….\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈8.222… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(8.222…) \\\\ p & ≈1.917….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the approximate order of the method is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing GTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22783af8-4a41-5a89-b104-4ac67b4fc43f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327651} {:answer/id #uuid "d4210ce1-16de-556e-b34a-2214fdca9d99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327652} {:answer/id #uuid "e560707e-91fc-5f7c-a9dd-40639c55c9b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327653} {:answer/id #uuid "7eef39f2-6a1f-54e9-b106-7a22dd086789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327654} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2b2e00-462f-5e81-9c7f-6cf9419818c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eef39f2-6a1f-54e9-b106-7a22dd086789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327654} :answer-field/id #uuid "78cd4ffd-117a-59ee-ba69-0a28c5130a06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "319b9230-63e8-5408-87a0-7bc7fecf8b48" :question/math-academy-id "q-334546" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.18,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.18)=0.486.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.06)=0.006.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.18$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.18}{0.06}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={3}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.486$ to $0.006.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.486}{0.006}=81.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈81 \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(81) \\\\ p & ≈4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximate order of the method is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing GTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4c63bc9-ec53-5aab-a5a4-0095958f362d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327658} {:answer/id #uuid "5adce8fa-c3c0-5542-94bc-aef951cf9156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.257" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327659} {:answer/id #uuid "e11a15d7-8d04-5ee8-aba7-c5c0d0ee46c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.042" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327660} {:answer/id #uuid "1f98e1ad-c6f2-5db2-8696-cd2f0ea4bcc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.181" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327661} {:answer/id #uuid "b9760ee4-dea1-534e-af74-eb262818e9ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.094" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f98e1ad-c6f2-5db2-8696-cd2f0ea4bcc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.181" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327661} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebc04528-12aa-53e2-b7d3-e56af792d02e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327657} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4a030f4-bb17-57c5-924e-1825e6e39805" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327664} {:answer/