 :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327669} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e5dc91a-ab23-5955-9902-a2c5525c00f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327676} {:answer/id #uuid "14dca13b-2e02-5091-9968-18680a1160e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327677} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb8aa47-8e54-51da-a6ea-ae64a2214a5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327678} {:answer/id #uuid "9da66781-8ec8-5fc6-bc9f-d87b0740392e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327679} {:answer/id #uuid "be2e4a2a-83e5-51f0-9d89-3195c29a97c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9da66781-8ec8-5fc6-bc9f-d87b0740392e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327679} :answer-field/id #uuid "d57cac1e-3d10-5339-bca7-4315cd2f49f9" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db1b2cc2-9ed0-52dd-b570-f28962fe5ef0" :question/math-academy-id "q-334567" :question/problem "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=2(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.6) & =2(0.6)^{4}-(0.6)^{5} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.129\\,6-1\\cdot 0.077\\,76 \\\\  & =0.259\\,2-0.077\\,76 \\\\  & =0.181\\,44 \\\\  & ≈0.181.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =2(0.2)^{4}-(0.2)^{5} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.001\\,6-1\\cdot 0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.003\\,2-0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.002\\,88 \\\\  & ≈0.003.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.2}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={3}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.181\\,44$ to $0.002\\,88.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.181\\,44}{0.002\\,88}=63≈63.000.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈63 \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(63) \\\\ p & ≈3.771….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=2(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,e(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327681 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing GTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "869b5c1a-98c2-59ef-854d-79794d318318" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.207" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327683} {:answer/id #uuid "f80a6bfa-1d70-5a8b-8aa7-ba429bbfcccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.032" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327684} {:answer/id #uuid "f4539f9b-d16b-5117-ae70-a49e4b3a7b0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.514" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327685} {:answer/id #uuid "87d73130-c059-5091-a54c-8f869f5cc406" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327686} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff14842-247e-5a10-90ad-5926a5091567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.321" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f80a6bfa-1d70-5a8b-8aa7-ba429bbfcccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.032" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327684} :answer-field/id #uuid "e14ceace-04ce-5f64-bd27-04a958bbad06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327682} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50fe2988-cf96-51e9-bae7-4aa16129cc4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.207" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327689} {:answer/id #uuid "95f9fe52-0f9a-5e56-b4a9-2564d525d4c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.032" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327690} {:answer/id #uuid "12924c42-b657-52eb-86e8-dc864f4a9b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.514" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327691} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee950b4-cde7-520b-981f-2ae11365b1fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327692} {:answer/id #uuid "5641c700-3f89-50d3-bdd0-7f2df0391177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.321" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ee950b4-cde7-520b-981f-2ae11365b1fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327692} :answer-field/id #uuid "aedf074d-a4f3-5166-b7a9-e106d2702d94" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327688} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2778a2b0-b994-576c-a7d3-6c1808505340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22.656" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327695} {:answer/id #uuid "c73d7341-1ac3-5e15-8171-44b8c642653f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "151.234" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327696} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4376d4-f499-5dfc-b1be-504667fe72df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.612" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327697} {:answer/id #uuid "4df73c2b-3efd-5275-bc54-fe68d22d162b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "134.431" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327698} {:answer/id #uuid "51e09300-c206-5346-aeca-f48b485a9b0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60.938" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4df73c2b-3efd-5275-bc54-fe68d22d162b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "134.431" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327698} :answer-field/id #uuid "501d56f2-c893-5472-bcbd-f4a9efbcfa82" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327694} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70aea0cf-4ce7-5ac7-826d-905aee59ce6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327701} {:answer/id #uuid "dd12ad40-8226-5741-89ba-eefbc39c30c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327702} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f2a48f-3ae9-51dd-8277-92fe3cad0e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327703} {:answer/id #uuid "1b5024fc-cd24-5c97-b40b-09f9b9e93b14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327704} {:answer/id #uuid "3f79e19a-842d-51cc-a5e4-441cdbf5b51c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b5024fc-cd24-5c97-b40b-09f9b9e93b14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327704} :answer-field/id #uuid "db35c2d6-e656-5678-84ec-8f59454cf94e" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9507c1b-a15f-58d3-89ee-30f737181818" :question/math-academy-id "q-334569" :question/problem "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.7,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.7$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.7,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.7) & =5(0.7)^{4}-(0.7)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.2401-1\\cdot 0.168\\,07 \\\\  & =1.200\\,5-0.168\\,07 \\\\  & =1.032\\,43 \\\\  & ≈1.032.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =5(0.2)^{4}-(0.2)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.001\\,6-1\\cdot 0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.008-0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.007\\,68 \\\\  & ≈0.008.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.7$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.7}{0.2}=3.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}=(3.5)^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $1.032\\,43$ to $0.007\\,68.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{1.032\\,43}{0.007\\,68}=134.430\\,99…≈134.431.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3.5)^{p} & ≈134.430\\,99… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3.5}⁡(134.430\\,99…) \\\\ p & ≈3.912….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,e(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327706 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d96bb35f-8d80-5835-9a31-288874061b4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327708} {:answer/id #uuid "46a926f8-6569-5cc1-b1a1-19c689764027" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327709} {:answer/id #uuid "73d75e1a-c18d-5436-8d73-ebf35a228889" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327710} {:answer/id #uuid "1f40bd7d-b60e-56b4-a894-f35111596526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327711} {:answer/id #uuid "d45f6218-9aa4-5971-abf4-29912c091340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d96bb35f-8d80-5835-9a31-288874061b4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327708} :answer-field/id #uuid "80e67794-f7e0-5374-a5a5-abf39cccee4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "acf7fb13-fc73-55d5-a726-fef5e6c5dbfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-334644" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.25)=0.50.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.10)=0.01.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.25$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.25}{0.10}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(2.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.50$ to $0.01.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.50}{0.01}=50.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.5)^{p+1} & ≈50 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(50) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.269… \\\\ p & ≈3.269….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6f30b62-dcc1-5673-937c-1ad6e26aea5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327715} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9e2b2a-325f-514a-96d4-e9c59787ef53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327716} {:answer/id #uuid "4abd6296-2907-5318-8ad0-4483f1d6c2fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327717} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb4c304-b27e-59a6-88cb-98118bebe98f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327718} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6a78d0-2892-5742-950f-ca436afb7f32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbb4c304-b27e-59a6-88cb-98118bebe98f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327718} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e076e5c-67dc-5d81-a19b-b322e047552c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be7eeba5-8d10-5737-b3ca-fb43e58066a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-334650" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.20,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.20)=0.78.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08)=0.008.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.20$ to $0.08.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.20}{0.08}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(2.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.78$ to $0.008.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.78}{0.008}=97.5.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.5)^{p+1} & ≈97.5 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(97.5) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.99… \\\\ p & ≈3.99….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ede15536-dad0-5146-a759-65fc29cf9c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.016" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327722} {:answer/id #uuid "9b82ee08-4936-573e-bc73-f7402c90f9e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.281" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327723} {:answer/id #uuid "cee7c395-d278-5aed-8186-75bc553abdf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327724} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c331c3-e683-5479-af13-507bd57b5f6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327725} {:answer/id #uuid "fe285bce-f31a-525b-80d8-5fd395fdda40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.550" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b82ee08-4936-573e-bc73-f7402c90f9e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.281" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327723} :answer-field/id #uuid "7219ad08-2b52-5866-9d87-e2f5696e00a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327721} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9343dd03-2012-5764-8066-71a044cb078d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327728} {:answer/id #uuid "5a8320ff-7486-5896-88bc-e95dd3ae7baa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327729} {:answer/id #uuid "7317aa31-1821-51c5-acb2-c295a3953f41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.281" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327730} {:answer/id #uuid "15d341cf-ad70-53d5-b3f3-ab620bd57c85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327731} {:answer/id #uuid "69fc4910-8001-54c3-b5a1-26a9a8bac228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.550" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a8320ff-7486-5896-88bc-e95dd3ae7baa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327729} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ed4085f-cef8-5b44-9a48-d68ca781f69c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327727} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea9279fe-cdd3-5764-89c2-9e85975460a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "99.068" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327734} {:answer/id #uuid "5aae55a8-a6ec-511f-8a8c-469687ecddf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "102.385" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327735} {:answer/id #uuid "fa55cc7f-dc2b-547b-8baf-9124912b2ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120.050" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327736} {:answer/id #uuid "9bba4a9d-4588-5e2e-b467-f7804e14724d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "107.403" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327737} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c32460-4da3-5909-80b2-928d1145c76d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "104.632" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5c32460-4da3-5909-80b2-928d1145c76d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "104.632" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327738} :answer-field/id #uuid "907645c3-2532-5dcc-97cb-88f9f76ab2dc" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327733} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e07a3d8c-1708-5bc7-b136-98d56a9e7240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327740} {:answer/id #uuid "637de3d3-9092-5cc1-b16c-525e0fd5826c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327741} {:answer/id #uuid "d4124e27-3416-5abd-adb0-4e59a2f5d83e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327742} {:answer/id #uuid "39816dd5-6189-5a8f-9ffa-af1a52eb6ff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327743} {:answer/id #uuid "760e6ce8-1d9a-5109-8eac-afe01148380b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39816dd5-6189-5a8f-9ffa-af1a52eb6ff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327743} :answer-field/id #uuid "a40345ef-436c-50fd-85c8-dc9cee8a1446" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c78fbb9-dc1c-5d53-be56-9ae880822440" :question/math-academy-id "q-334724" :question/problem "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=8(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.5$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.5) & =8(0.5)^{5}+2(0.5)^{6} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 0.031\\,25+2\\cdot 0.015\\,625 \\\\  & =0.281\\,25 \\\\  & ≈0.281.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.2) & =8(0.2)^{5}+2(0.2)^{6} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 0.000\\,32+2\\cdot 0.000\\,064 \\\\  & =0.002\\,688 \\\\  & ≈0.003.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.2}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2.5}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,25$ to $0.002\\,688.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.281\\,25}{0.002\\,688}=104.631\\,69…≈104.632.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2.5}^{p+1} & ≈104.631\\,69… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(104.631\\,69…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈5.075… \\\\ p & ≈4.075….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=8(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{5})=O\\,((Δx)^{4+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9dfb73f-753f-517e-a9fb-21d03f7bdb68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327747} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ab14e2-4a0d-5806-be60-34f002513326" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327748} {:answer/id #uuid "2d7b88ea-814f-5b4a-81c1-4c220ead7a1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327749} {:answer/id #uuid "4b418b3c-6ebe-5714-ab1f-b37e5f6f7ed0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327750} {:answer/id #uuid "1fa2c886-2977-50d9-8bdf-001e2068384b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d7b88ea-814f-5b4a-81c1-4c220ead7a1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327749} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba6975a0-81ff-5083-b52b-d9573759633c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd19aa16-bd3c-58a1-8c30-53875415e31f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334752" :question/problem "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{6}+5(Δx)^{7}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{9}$\n- $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}+3(Δx)^{8}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{6}+5(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{6}=2(Δx)^{5+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $5$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{9},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4(Δx)^{4}=0.4(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}+3(Δx)^{8},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{5}=6(Δx)^{4+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a088d48-5e47-5382-aa08-0325ce6d72a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327754} {:answer/id #uuid "9e95f7aa-a745-57a0-8cf4-d938df8f6935" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327755} {:answer/id #uuid "b389cab2-519b-50f9-b4e6-134a9a2be56c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327756} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb41ea8-be39-5c1f-a2a6-bbb7131771ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327757} {:answer/id #uuid "6712c7d4-2dcb-5737-bf5a-5a06740293ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b389cab2-519b-50f9-b4e6-134a9a2be56c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327756} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef86bc0-98d4-513d-a96a-d08ef51a4dd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ec85f81-14c8-5630-88d5-149a1aa199ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-334755" :question/problem "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a third-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+6(Δx)^{6}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.8(Δx)^{2}+5(Δx)^{3}$\n- $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+6(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{3}=4(Δx)^{2+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.8(Δx)^{2}+5(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.8(Δx)^{2}=0.8(Δx)^{1+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $9(Δx)^{4}=9(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eda904b3-6fe1-59dd-b607-f8948d819c0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327761} {:answer/id #uuid "092df854-b2b4-5c77-a7f0-5876627e1b93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327762} {:answer/id #uuid "1266b73c-6ba8-5d56-89d4-e29b4473f5d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327763} {:answer/id #uuid "1e4e036e-1c3a-528f-8885-894b1ef8a84d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327764} {:answer/id #uuid "b94d69b0-f450-56f1-82e8-2c14ae3b6a7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eda904b3-6fe1-59dd-b607-f8948d819c0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327761} :answer-field/id #uuid "97edc09a-d4fc-5e52-a3c4-fa4c8f799b9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3b62faef-4f01-500f-8e53-f446a4f9378d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334757" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-3(Δx)^{6}$\n- $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{5}+(Δx)^{7}$" :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-3(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{4}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{3}=2(Δx)^{3}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{5}+(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4(Δx)^{5}=0.4(Δx)^{4+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0082a3a-8299-5ef6-b970-f615488e202f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.002" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0082a3a-8299-5ef6-b970-f615488e202f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.002" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327768} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3d11f04-eb9f-53fd-8818-3d2a90fa1664" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a10b1f6-9269-53d3-ade8-daedb2805249" :question/math-academy-id "q-334781" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$\nSuppose that using step size $Δx=0.1$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.1)≈0.512.$\nIf we instead use a step size $Δx=0.025,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.025)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.1$ to $0.025.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.1}{0.025}=4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4}={4}^{4}=256.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.025,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.025)≈\\frac{0.512}{256}=0.002.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb075d45-059a-5c30-acad-226263ddce95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327771} {:answer/id #uuid "b1872365-4257-544a-9736-ee9615954847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1872365-4257-544a-9736-ee9615954847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327772} :answer-field/id #uuid "06a3953a-69eb-5039-b896-19ef58a264cd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5dcc6b3a-f703-5336-b91c-1153696cc28c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334796" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $2.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.15$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.15)≈0.0225.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.10,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.10)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.15$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.15}{0.10}=1.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $2,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}=(1.5)^{2}=2.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.10,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈\\frac{0.0225}{2.25}=0.01.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6bfd448-8c77-542e-913b-b1d82ce527a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6bfd448-8c77-542e-913b-b1d82ce527a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327775} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bb172d8-b607-5d07-9b84-e6786163d41f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "276d68fa-4cd1-5416-b700-dfeb8978eba6" :question/math-academy-id "q-334827" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $2.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.18$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.18)≈0.2025.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.04,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.04)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.18$ to $0.04.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.18}{0.04}=4.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $2,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}=(4.5)^{2}=20.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.04,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.04)≈\\frac{0.2025}{20.25}=0.01.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327776 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "059cf0dc-c6e7-518f-b6be-7aac96e2ac41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "059cf0dc-c6e7-518f-b6be-7aac96e2ac41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327778} :answer-field/id #uuid "59973590-9335-59e3-b25b-131f52b69d9a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4cf6dc3-6e78-578e-a0e6-3564f50cd0db" :question/math-academy-id "q-334946" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $1.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.5$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.5)≈0.2.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.25,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.25)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.25.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.25}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $1,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{1+1}={2}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.25,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.25)≈\\frac{0.2}{4}=0.05.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86b1ad47-a165-51f7-a7ca-5284bb890409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327781} {:answer/id #uuid "1df165d1-2468-5ad7-ab0d-3674062ac289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.004" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1df165d1-2468-5ad7-ab0d-3674062ac289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.004" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327782} :answer-field/id #uuid "a21c3182-2cc2-508b-9dd7-d3980c60c0c5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ead683db-5726-5670-bf85-2080e4a803bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-334948" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $2.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.3$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.3)≈0.108.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.3$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.3}{0.1}=3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $2,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{2+1}={3}^{3}=27.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.1)≈\\frac{0.108}{27}=0.004.\n$$"}]}
